    "plan": {
      "schemaVersion": 1,
      "depth": "brief",
      "title": "The Upward Throw",
      "story": "When you toss a ball straight up into the air, it leaves your hand with a certain speed and immediately begins to slow down. Gravity pulls it downward, steadily reducing its upward speed until it stops for a brief fraction of a second at its highest point. From there, it begins its fall back down, picking up speed until it reaches your hand again. Let's see how its speed and height change during this flight.",
      "cardDescription": "Explore how an upward throw works under gravity. See how initial speed determines the apex height and total flight time.",
      "learningGoal": "Say that gravity still pulls the ball down at the top of its throw, where it is still for an instant.",
      "instructions": "Adjust the launch speed and press play to observe the motion. Use the scrubber or the top button to inspect key moments.",
      "supportingVisuals": [],
      "scene": {
        "description": "One moving ball on a fixed height scale with a launch-height guide; the scale does not change with the launch speed, so a faster toss visibly rises higher. A blue Motion arrow beside the ball shortens on the way up, is absent at the top and points down on the way back. A separate dashed warm gravity arrow keeps the same direction and length throughout. Beside the ball a callout (frame object id callout.toss) names the motion and shows gravity. The scene starts at launch and runs the whole rise and fall. No second ball, graph, equation, number readout or comparison strip.",
